from common import Batch
b=Batch('REACT',1)
def q(*args,**kw): b.add(*args,**kw)
q('C','Rendering','Component purity','What makes a React component’s render function pure?','Its output depends on its inputs. It does not mutate preexisting external state during render. Repeating render with the same inputs should not produce new observable side effects.','React may render more than once or abandon work. Network requests and external mutations during render therefore have no reliable commit lifecycle.','Rendering may be repeated.','Would replaying the function change the outside world?','react:learn/keeping-components-pure')
q('M','JSX','Passing event handlers','Which JSX passes a handler to run on click rather than invoking it during render?','<button onClick={save}>Save</button>','React expects a function for onClick. Calling save() while producing JSX runs it immediately; returning another function would be a different explicit contract.','Pass behavior, not its result.','Where are the call parentheses?','react:learn/responding-to-events',wrong=['<button onClick={save()}>Save</button>','<button onClick="save">Save</button>','<button onClick={undefined}>Save</button>'])
q('B','State','Direct object mutation','Why might this name edit fail to update the UI, and which setter call fixes it?','It reuses the mutated state object. Use: setUser(previous => ({ ...previous, name: "Ada" }));','The assignment mutates the current snapshot.~The setter receives the same object identity and may be skipped.~An immutable replacement communicates the change without corrupting prior snapshots.','State is a snapshot.','Is the next object a new identity?','react:learn/updating-objects-in-state','user.name = "Ada";\nsetUser(user);')
q('C','State','State snapshot','Why does reading count immediately after setCount(count + 1) still see the old count in that handler?','The handler belongs to one render’s snapshot. The setter queues a later state update. It does not rewrite the local count binding in the running handler.','A setter is not synchronous assignment to a local variable. Compute the intended next value locally when that same handler needs it.','A render creates local bindings.','Can a setter rewrite an existing closure?','react:learn/state-as-a-snapshot')
q('B','State','Repeated replacement updates','Starting at zero, this click only adds one. Correct it to add three using queued updates.','Each call replaces state with the same snapshot-derived value. Replace each call with: setCount(previous => previous + 1);','All three expressions read the same count.~React queues three replacements with 1.~Functional updaters consume the preceding queued value, producing 1, 2 and 3. Keep updater functions pure.','Look at each queued value.','Can each update receive the latest pending state?','react:learn/queueing-a-series-of-state-updates','setCount(count + 1);\nsetCount(count + 1);\nsetCount(count + 1);')
q('M','Props','Read-only inputs','A child needs to change its parent’s selectedId. Which approach follows React’s data flow?','Call a callback prop supplied by the parent.','The parent owns its state and passes data down. Mutating a prop or assigning to a local parameter does not request a parent update.','Find the state owner.','How can a child request a change?','react:learn/passing-props-to-a-component',wrong=['Assign directly to props.selectedId.','Change a destructured selectedId local variable.','Store the new value on the JSX element object.'])
q('B','Lists','Stable keys','Rows keep the wrong local input state after reordering. Correct the key choice.','Index keys associate state with positions. Use: <Row key={item.id} item={item} />;','React matches siblings by type and key.~Reordering with index keys preserves positional identities.~Stable record IDs let state follow the corresponding records; keys must be unique among siblings.','Keys identify logical items.','Does position remain stable after reorder?','react:learn/rendering-lists#keeping-list-items-in-order-with-key','items.map((item, index) => (\n  <Row key={index} item={item} />\n));')
q('C','Rendering','Render and commit','How do React’s render and commit phases differ?','Render calculates the next UI description. Commit applies the necessary host changes. Effects synchronize external systems in relation to committed work, not every attempted render.','A render can be restarted or discarded. Treating every component call as a DOM update confuses calculation with committed changes.','Separate calculation from application.','Can calculated work be abandoned?','react:learn/render-and-commit')
q('W','Forms','Controlled text input','Write a complete NameField component with state-controlled text and an associated visible label.','import { useId, useState } from "react";\n\nexport default function NameField() {\n  const id = useId();\n  const [name, setName] = useState("");\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <label htmlFor={id}>Name</label>\n      <input id={id} value={name}\n        onChange={event => setName(event.target.value)} />\n    </div>\n  );\n}','Initialize the value as a string.~Each change copies the input value into state.~The next render controls the input and useId connects its label. Do not switch the value between undefined and a string.','The value must have one owner.','Pair value with a change handler.','react:reference/react-dom/components/input','// Implement NameField as a function component.')
q('S','Forms','Uncontrolled initial value','A form should let the DOM own an input after giving it an initial name. Which React prop fits, and how is it read on submit?','Use defaultValue for the initial text. Read the submitted field through FormData or a ref. Changing defaultValue later is not the controlled-input update model.','value makes the field controlled and needs appropriate updates. defaultValue establishes initial content without continuously owning the current value.','Initial and current values differ.','Which prop specifies only initialization?','react:reference/react-dom/components/input#providing-an-initial-value-for-an-input')
q('B','JSX','Falsy numeric rendering','Why does an empty list display 0, and what fixes the condition?','The && expression returns numeric zero. Use: {items.length > 0 && <List items={items} />};','When length is zero, && evaluates to zero.~React renders numeric zero as text.~A boolean comparison produces false, which React omits.','&& returns an operand.','Is zero omitted like false?','react:learn/conditional-rendering#logical-and-operator-','return <div>{items.length && <List items={items} />}</div>;')
q('M','Hooks','Hook call order','Where should an ordinary useState call appear?','At the top level of a component or custom Hook.','React tracks ordinary Hooks by consistent call order. Conditional calls and loops can change that order between renders; event callbacks are not component renders.','Hook order must be stable.','Could this location run a different number of times?','react:reference/rules/rules-of-hooks',wrong=['Inside a conditional after an early return.','Inside an onClick callback.','Inside a loop whose length depends on props.'])
q('C','Events','Event propagation','How do event.stopPropagation and event.preventDefault differ in a React click handler?','stopPropagation stops further propagation of that event. preventDefault cancels its cancelable default action. Neither operation is a substitute for the other.','A link can still navigate after propagation is stopped. Preventing navigation does not necessarily stop parent click handlers.','Separate listeners from browser behavior.','Is navigation a listener or a default action?','react:learn/responding-to-events#preventing-default-behavior')
q('W','Forms','Checkbox state','Write a complete controlled checkbox component labeled Subscribe, initially unchecked.','import { useState } from "react";\n\nexport default function Subscribe() {\n  const [checked, setChecked] = useState(false);\n  return (\n    <label>\n      <input type="checkbox" checked={checked}\n        onChange={event => setChecked(event.target.checked)} />\n      Subscribe\n    </label>\n  );\n}','Control checked with a boolean.~Read event.target.checked on each change.~Using value would read the submission value rather than the checked state.','Checkboxes have a dedicated boolean property.','Which event target field reflects selection?','react:reference/react-dom/components/input#reading-the-input-values-when-submitting-a-form','// Implement Subscribe as a function component.')
q('C','State','Lifting state','Two sibling filters must show the same selection. Where should that shared state live?','Move it to their closest appropriate common owner. Pass the current selection and change callbacks to both. Keep a single source of truth for the shared value.','Duplicated sibling state needs synchronization and can drift. Lifting everything globally is unnecessary when a nearby owner can coordinate it.','Locate the common owner.','Can both children receive the same value?','react:learn/sharing-state-between-components')
q('M','Lists','key is not a prop','A Row receives key={item.id}. How should it also receive that ID for use inside Row?','Pass a separate id prop.','React uses key for reconciliation and does not forward it as a normal prop. A separate prop communicates the data explicitly.','Some JSX attributes are special.','Does reconciliation metadata reach props?','react:learn/rendering-lists#rules-of-keys',wrong=['Read props.key directly.','Read key from useState automatically.','Use props.children.key regardless of children.'])
q('S','State','Reset by identity','An editor should discard its draft when the selected document changes. How can a key express that behavior?','Render the editor with key={documentId}. A different key makes React treat it as a different component instance. Its local state resets instead of being preserved at that position.','Changing ordinary props does not inherently reset state. A key reset also resets other state and effects in that subtree, so the boundary should be deliberate.','State belongs to tree identity.','What happens when identity changes?','react:learn/preserving-and-resetting-state#resetting-state-with-a-key')
q('B','State','Expensive initialization','Why does this expensive initializer run on every render, and what is the corrected Hook call?','The function is called before useState receives its argument. Use: const [items, setItems] = useState(buildItems);','JavaScript evaluates buildItems() on every component call.~Passing the function lets React invoke it for initialization.~Development Strict Mode may invoke the initializer twice to check purity.','Inspect argument evaluation.','Can the Hook receive a function instead of its result?','react:reference/react/useState#avoiding-recreating-the-initial-state','const [items, setItems] = useState(buildItems());')
q('C','JSX','Children composition','Why might a reusable Card accept children instead of hard-coding a particular form inside it?','children lets callers supply nested content. Card can own the shared visual structure. The content remains composed by the caller rather than selected by a growing list of special-case props.','Composition separates the wrapper’s responsibility from the content’s behavior. children is still ordinary React data and does not imply independent state sharing.','Separate container and content.','What should the caller choose?','react:learn/passing-props-to-a-component#passing-jsx-as-children')
q('M','Effects','External synchronization','Which task most clearly belongs in an Effect?','Connecting a component to an external chat subscription.','An Effect synchronizes with an external system and can return cleanup. Deriving display text from props usually belongs in render; user-triggered operations often belong in their event handler.','Identify the external system.','Does work need to track mounting and dependencies?','react:learn/synchronizing-with-effects',wrong=['Concatenating firstName and lastName for display.','Sorting a copied array for rendering.','Computing whether a supplied list is empty.'])
q('B','Forms','Controlled undefined value','This input starts uncontrolled and later becomes controlled. What should its initialization be when it always represents text?','Initialize with an empty string: const [name, setName] = useState("");','An undefined value does not establish a controlled text value.~After typing, a string switches the ownership mode.~Start with a string and keep the value a string throughout the component lifetime.','Keep input ownership consistent.','What empty value still has type string?','react:reference/react-dom/components/input#im-getting-an-error-a-component-is-changing-an-uncontrolled-input-to-be-controlled','const [name, setName] = useState();\nreturn <input value={name} onChange={e => setName(e.target.value)} />;')
q('C','Refs','State versus ref','When should a timer handle live in a ref instead of state?','Use a ref when changing the handle should not affect rendered output. The ref persists across renders. Updating its current field does not schedule a render.','State is for values whose changes should drive the UI. A ref is mutable storage, so reading arbitrary current values during render can undermine predictable rendering.','Does this value affect the UI?','Should assigning it cause a render?','react:reference/react/useRef')
q('W','State','Toggle updater','Write a complete Toggle component whose button alternates between On and Off.','import { useState } from "react";\n\nexport default function Toggle() {\n  const [on, setOn] = useState(false);\n  return (\n    <button type="button" aria-pressed={on}\n      onClick={() => setOn(previous => !previous)}>\n      {on ? "On" : "Off"}\n    </button>\n  );\n}','Initialize the boolean state.~The updater negates the pending value.~The next render updates text and pressed semantics. The updater stays pure even if invoked again in development.','The next value depends on the previous one.','Use a functional state update.','react:reference/react/useState#updating-state-based-on-the-previous-state','// Implement Toggle as a function component.')
q('U','Lists','Key scope','Follow-up to REACT-007: must a record key be globally unique across every list on the page?','No, keys need to be unique among siblings. The same ID may appear in separate lists. Within one sibling set, stable unique keys are needed to match items correctly.','Keys are interpreted in their parent’s child set. A global key registry is unnecessary and random keys destroy stability.','Identity is contextual.','Which parent compares these children?','react:learn/rendering-lists#rules-of-keys')
q('S','Development','Strict Mode checks','Why can a pure component appear to render twice in development Strict Mode?','Strict Mode intentionally repeats selected work to expose impure logic. This is a development check. Fix the impurity instead of relying on an exact render count.','The extra invocation is not proof that two DOM commits occurred. Production behavior and development diagnostics should not be conflated.','Look for a development-only check.','Is the repeated function supposed to be pure?','react:reference/react/StrictMode#fixing-bugs-found-by-double-rendering-in-development')
q('B','Effects','Missing dependency','The subscription keeps using the first roomId. Identify the bug and correct the dependencies.','roomId is a reactive dependency. Use: }, [roomId]); for the Effect’s closing line.','The empty list ties the Effect to its initial captured roomId.~When roomId changes, no cleanup or reconnect occurs.~Including roomId disconnects the old room before connecting the new one.','Effects capture render values.','What change should trigger reconnection?','react:reference/react/useEffect#specifying-reactive-dependencies','useEffect(() => {\n  const connection = connect(roomId);\n  connection.start();\n  return () => connection.stop();\n}, []);')
q('C','Effects','Cleanup sequence','When an Effect dependency changes, in what order do cleanup and setup run?','React runs the old cleanup using the old captured values. It then runs the new setup using the new values. Final unmount also cleans up the active Effect.','Cleanup is not limited to unmount. Development Strict Mode can additionally run a setup-cleanup-setup check, so cleanup must undo setup.','Think of replacing one synchronization.','Which subscription must stop first?','react:reference/react/useEffect#parameters')
q('F','Effects','Interval stale closure','Replace this Counter with a working component that increments every second and cleans up its timer.','import { useEffect, useState } from "react";\n\nexport default function Counter() {\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const id = setInterval(() => setCount(n => n + 1), 1000);\n    return () => clearInterval(id);\n  }, []);\n  return <output>{count}</output>;\n}','The original callback captures the initial count.~A functional updater reads the pending count instead.~Cleanup clears the interval on unmount and during development checks. Timers are not exact clocks.','The callback outlives its render.','Can the setter supply fresh state?','react:reference/react/useEffect#updating-state-based-on-previous-state-from-an-effect','function Counter() {\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\n  useEffect(() => {\n    setInterval(() => setCount(count + 1), 1000);\n  }, []);\n  return <output>{count}</output>;\n}')
q('B','Effects','Unstable object dependency','Why does this connection restart on every render, and which change removes the unnecessary dependency?','options is a new object each render. Create it inside the Effect and depend on [roomId] instead.','Dependency comparison uses Object.is.~The new object fails identity comparison each render.~Moving construction into setup makes roomId the actual reactive input. Do not omit roomId.','Compare object identities.','Where can the setup-only object be created?','react:reference/react/useEffect#removing-unnecessary-object-dependencies','const options = { roomId };\nuseEffect(() => {\n  const connection = connect(options);\n  return () => connection.close();\n}, [options]);')
q('S','Effects','Derived state avoidance','A component stores fullName in state and updates it in an Effect whenever firstName or lastName changes. How should it be simplified?','Compute fullName from firstName and lastName during render. Remove the redundant state and synchronization Effect. Memoize only if an actually expensive computation warrants it.','An Effect creates an avoidable render with stale derived state followed by another render. Pure derivation keeps the value consistent with its inputs.','Can the value be calculated directly?','Is there an external system to synchronize?','react:learn/you-might-not-need-an-effect#updating-state-based-on-props-or-state')
q('M','Effects','Dependency comparison','How does React compare each entry in an Effect dependency list?','Using Object.is against the previous entry.','React does not recursively compare object fields. A fresh array or object can retrigger an Effect even when its contents look identical.','Identity matters for objects.','Which equality algorithm is documented?','react:reference/react/useEffect',wrong=['Using deep recursive equality.','Using JSON.stringify on the entire list.','Using loose equality with coercion.'])
q('W','Refs','Focus an input','Write a complete SearchFocus component with an input and a button that focuses it.','import { useRef } from "react";\n\nexport default function SearchFocus() {\n  const inputRef = useRef(null);\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <input ref={inputRef} aria-label="Search" />\n      <button type="button" onClick={() => inputRef.current?.focus()}>\n        Focus search\n      </button>\n    </div>\n  );\n}','React assigns the committed input node to the ref.~The click handler focuses that node.~The optional check handles a missing node; manipulating focus during render would be premature.','DOM nodes arrive after commit.','Use the ref in the event handler.','react:learn/manipulating-the-dom-with-refs','// Implement SearchFocus as a function component.')
q('C','Refs','Ref mutation visibility','Why does incrementing ref.current not refresh a displayed count?','Ref mutation does not request a render. React therefore has no state update to display. Use state for a count that must be reflected in the UI.','A later unrelated render might reveal the mutated value, which can hide the design problem. That does not make refs reactive state.','Persistence and reactivity differ.','What schedules a render?','react:learn/referencing-values-with-refs#differences-between-refs-and-state')
q('B','Hooks','Conditional early return','What Hook-order bug appears when enabled changes, and how should the Hook be positioned?','useState is skipped when disabled. Move the useState call before the conditional return.','One render can return without calling the Hook.~Another render calls it, changing the Hook sequence.~Call Hooks consistently and make returned UI conditional afterward.','Compare both render paths.','Do all paths call the same Hooks?','react:reference/rules/rules-of-hooks','function Panel({ enabled }) {\n  if (!enabled) return null;\n  const [open, setOpen] = useState(false);\n  return <button onClick={() => setOpen(!open)}>{String(open)}</button>;\n}')
q('T','Reducers','Pure reducer execution','This is ordinary JavaScript using a React-style reducer. What does it log?','3 0','The reducer returns a new state with count increased by amount.~The original state is not mutated.~The output shows both states. Reducers must remain pure when React invokes them.','Track old and new objects.','Is state assigned to directly?','react:reference/react/useReducer','function reducer(state, action) {\n  if (action.type === "add") {\n    return { ...state, count: state.count + action.amount };\n  }\n  return state;\n}\nconst initial = { count: 0 };\nconst next = reducer(initial, { type: "add", amount: 3 });\nconsole.log(next.count, initial.count);')
q('C','Reducers','Choosing useReducer','When is useReducer useful compared with several independent useState calls?','It centralizes related state transitions. Actions describe what happened, and a reducer computes the next state. It is useful when updates must preserve shared invariants across several fields.','A reducer does not automatically provide global state or improve performance. Independent simple values can remain separate state variables.','Look for coordinated transitions.','Which fields must change together?','react:learn/extracting-state-logic-into-a-reducer')
q('B','Reducers','Reducer mutation','Why is this reducer unsafe, and what corrected return handles add?','It mutates the existing state. Use: return { ...state, count: state.count + 1 };','The original object is changed before being returned.~React may see the same identity and skip work.~Immutable calculation preserves previous snapshots and remains safe under repeated purity checks.','The reducer receives a previous snapshot.','Can it construct a new next snapshot?','react:reference/react/useReducer#ive-dispatched-an-action-but-the-screen-doesnt-update','function reducer(state, action) {\n  if (action.type === "add") {\n    state.count++;\n    return state;\n  }\n  return state;\n}')
q('M','Context','Nearest provider','When several providers supply the same context, which value does useContext read?','The closest matching provider above the calling component.','Context lookup follows ancestry, not DOM proximity or provider creation order. A provider returned by the same component is below its own Hook call.','Think about the React tree.','Is the provider above the consumer?','react:reference/react/useContext',wrong=['The first provider mounted anywhere in the app.','The provider returned later by the same component.','An average of every matching provider value.'])
q('S','Context','Provider identity churn','A context provider creates { user, logout } on every render. What performance concern should you investigate?','Its value identity changes on every render. Consumers of that context can update even when the meaningful data is unchanged. Consider stable callbacks, memoized values or split contexts after measuring.','React compares provider values with Object.is. memo on a consumer does not block updates caused by a context it reads.','Inspect the provider value identity.','Can unrelated state recreate the value?','react:reference/react/useContext#optimizing-re-renders-when-passing-objects-and-functions')
q('U','Context','Default context value','Follow-up to REACT-038: does createContext(defaultValue) supply defaultValue when an existing provider explicitly supplies undefined?','No, that provider’s undefined is the value. The default applies only when no matching provider exists above. Validate missing-provider cases separately if the API requires a provider.','A default value is not a nullish fallback for provider values. Explicit undefined and absent provider are distinct situations.','Distinguish absence from an explicit value.','Was a matching provider found?','react:reference/react/useContext#my-component-doesnt-see-the-value-from-my-provider')
q('C','Custom Hooks','Logic versus shared state','If two components call the same custom Hook containing useState, do they share its state?','No, each call has its own state. Custom Hooks share logic. Shared state needs a common owner or an external store.','Extracting code does not merge Hook instances. Module-level mutable state would be a different mechanism with different lifecycle concerns.','A Hook call is an instance.','How many state owners exist?','react:learn/reusing-logic-with-custom-hooks#custom-hooks-let-you-share-stateful-logic-not-state-itself')
q('W','Custom Hooks','Reusable boolean state','Implement useToggle(initial = false) returning [value, toggle]. Keep toggle stable across renders.','import { useCallback, useState } from "react";\n\nexport function useToggle(initial = false) {\n  const [value, setValue] = useState(initial);\n  const toggle = useCallback(() => {\n    setValue(previous => !previous);\n  }, []);\n  return [value, toggle];\n}','Each Hook call owns its own state.~The stable callback uses a functional update and captures no changing state.~The initial argument initializes once; later initial changes do not reset state.','Avoid capturing the current value.','Can a functional update remove that dependency?','react:reference/react/useCallback#optimizing-a-custom-hook','// Implement useToggle(initial = false).')
q('M','Memoization','useMemo result','What does useMemo cache between renders when its dependencies are unchanged?','The result of its calculation function.','useMemo calls a calculation and caches its result. useCallback caches a function identity instead. Neither is required for ordinary correctness.','Function versus function result.','Which Hook invokes the calculation?','react:reference/react/useMemo',wrong=['Every render of all descendants.','The calculation function’s identity without invoking it.','All state updates inside the component.'])
q('S','Memoization','Avoid trivial memoization','Should every string concatenation in a component be wrapped in useMemo?','No, cheap calculations usually do not justify it. Measure a real performance problem before adding caching. Prefer simple pure render logic when memoization provides no benefit.','Memoization adds dependency management and retained values. It is a performance optimization, not a general correctness mechanism.','Compare saved work with overhead.','Is the calculation measurably expensive?','react:reference/react/useMemo#should-you-add-usememo-everywhere')
q('B','Memoization','Missing memo dependency','Why does this filtered list ignore a changed query, and which dependency list fixes it?','query is captured but omitted. Use: [items, query].','The cache stays valid according to the incomplete list.~Changing only query reuses the old result.~Include every reactive input to the calculation; mutating items in place is a separate identity problem.','List all values read by the calculation.','Which one can change independently?','react:reference/react/useMemo#parameters','const visible = useMemo(\n  () => items.filter(item => item.name.includes(query)),\n  [items]\n);')
q('C','Memoization','React.memo limitations','What can still render a memoized component even when its props compare equal?','Its own state updates can render it. Changes to context it consumes can also render it. memo is an optimization and is not a guarantee that rendering never occurs.','memo primarily compares props from a parent. It does not freeze the component or detach it from state and context.','Props are not the only input.','What does the component subscribe to?','react:reference/react/memo')
q('S','Memoization','Function prop stability','A memoized child rerenders because its parent passes a fresh callback. When might useCallback help?','It can preserve the callback identity while its dependencies are unchanged. That can let the child’s prop comparison succeed. It helps only if other props and consumed context are also sufficiently stable.','Caching a callback does not stop the parent from rendering. An always-new object prop can still defeat the child’s memoization.','Examine all compared props.','Is the callback the actual changing input?','react:reference/react/useCallback#skipping-re-rendering-of-components')
q('B','Effects','Async Effect callback','Why is an async function invalid as the Effect setup return contract, and how should asynchronous work be started?','An async setup returns a Promise, not cleanup. Use a synchronous setup that starts an inner async function and returns cleanup.','React invokes setup and expects undefined or a cleanup function.~An async function always returns a Promise.~Keep cancellation or stale-result protection in synchronous cleanup; handle promise rejection inside the async work.','Check what async functions return.','Can cleanup be returned synchronously?','react:reference/react/useEffect#fetching-data-with-effects','useEffect(async () => {\n  const data = await loadData();\n  setData(data);\n}, []);')
q('C','Effects','useLayoutEffect timing','When can useLayoutEffect be justified over useEffect?','Use it for layout measurement that must affect the UI before the browser paints. It runs after DOM changes and blocks repaint while it works. Prefer useEffect when that blocking timing is unnecessary.','Using layout Effects for general fetching does not make network work synchronous. Excessive blocking work delays visible updates.','Ask whether a visible intermediate layout is acceptable.','Must measurement happen before paint?','react:reference/react/useLayoutEffect')
q('U','Development','Effect replay cleanup','Follow-up to REACT-025: a development setup-cleanup-setup cycle creates duplicate connections. What should be fixed?','Make cleanup fully undo the connection created by setup. Each setup should own the resource it returns cleanup for. After replay, only the currently active connection should remain.','A ref guard that merely skips the second setup hides missing cleanup and can break real remounts. The lifecycle must remain correct under replacement.','Track resource ownership.','Does each setup have a matching teardown?','react:reference/react/StrictMode#fixing-bugs-found-by-re-running-effects-in-development')
b.save('batch04')
